The 95\% HC0 interval of the regression covers the true GATE in 73.6\% of the 1800 Bernoulli cells of Figure~\ref{fig:cov}. Coverage is set by the size of the bias relative to the SD and not by leverage: the mean is 93\%, 92\%, 87\%, 67\% and 16\% for $|{\rm bias}|/{\rm SD}$ in $\le0.25$, 0.25--0.5, 0.5--1, 1--2 and $>2$. Among cells with $|{\rm bias}|/{\rm SD}<0.25$ coverage is 94\%, 93\%, 90\% and 95\% by quartile of $L$: leverage costs a few points at most.

An interval widened by a bound on the unidentified part \citep{armstrong2020simple} covers only by being wide: on the 666 new cells of the confirmatory block it covers 0.99 (HC0 at 90\%: 0.70) at 8--14 times the width of HC0, no better than HC0 inflated to the same width (coverage difference $-0.010$, $[-0.020, -0.002]$ over 24 groups). The width is mostly noise in the estimated curvature (about 7, 25, 110 at $d=5$, 20, 80 with the response removed; the true curvature is at most 4.7); with the true curvature it is ten times narrower but covers 0.82, since the bias of (b) and (e) is not curvature (exploratory, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}). On the threshold mechanism outside the observed exposures, where we expected it to fail, it covers 0.998 of 27 cells (Appendix~\ref{app:extra}).
